  "rationale": "The plan cites NICE passages that discuss ROM risk thresholds (>24 h) and CRP usage, but the text attributes quantitative risk percentages, point values, specific urgency timing, and a 48‑72 h antibiotic course that are not found in the supplied evidence. Citations are therefore mismatched or incomplete. No numeric dosing is presented, so no ungrounded dose claim is identified.",
  "rubric_version": 4,
  "passage_catalog": {
    "S010": "Source: NICE\nSection: Why the committee made the recommendations\n\nIn 2026, evidence from 3 studies showed that the risk of early‑onset neonatal infection increased as the time between prelabour rupture of membranes (PROM) at term and birth increased. ... using their clinical experience, the committee decided to keep this at more than 24 hours.",
    "S011": "Source: NICE\nSection: How the recommendations might affect practice\n\nThe 2026 revised wording for the risk factor for early‑onset neonatal infection of rupture of membranes more than 24 hours before a term birth could increase the number of neonates requiring monitoring ...",
    "S013": "Source: NICE\nSection: Why the committee made the recommendations\n\nThe evidence was inconclusive ... committee decided to keep this at more than 24 hours. ... framework for making antibiotic decisions after birth ... retained from the 2012 guideline.",
    "S014": "Source: NICE\nSection: Why the committee made the recommendation\nRecommendation: rec1\n\nCRP results should be used alongside other factors; not sole determinant for antibiotics.",
    "S015": "Source: NICE\nSection: Why the committee made the recommendation\nRecommendation: rec1\n\nAdvice from experienced professionals should be available for treatment decisions.",
